Q: get element by Id Using Razor syntax A: <p>What are you trying to do once you have your <code>var nameTxtElement</code>? You could probably accomplish the scenario with Razor, but you might have to think a little differently than if you were using WebForms. For example, you could do something like this...</p>

<pre><code>@{ 
 //...
 var cssClass = "something"; 
 // ...
}

&lt;input id='namefield' type='text' class='@cssClass' /&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>... if you wanted to insert a CSS class dynamically.</p>

Q: Cursor hidden on GLFW (borderless) fullscreen window when told not to A: <p>I dont think the cursor line is the actual problem here.</p>
<p>This:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>glfwSetInputMode(window, GLFW_CURSOR, GLFW_CURSOR_NORMAL);
</code></pre>
<p>is already the correct way to tell GLFW to use the normal visible cursor. So if this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::cout &lt;&lt; glfwGetInputMode(window, GLFW_CURSOR) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>prints <code>GLFW_CURSOR_NORMAL</code>, then GLFW is not hiding it on purpose.</p>
<p>The bigger issue is that your window is not really being created as fullscreen. You are doing this:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>window = glfwCreateWindow(WIDTH, HEIGHT, &quot;MV_Game&quot;, nullptr, nullptr);
</code></pre>
<p>Because the monitor argument is <code>nullptr</code>, this creates a normal windowed window. Then you remove decorations and move it to <code>(0, 0)</code>, which makes it look like borderless fullscreen, but it is still just a regular undecorated window.</p>
<p>Try creating it as an actual fullscreen window first:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>GLFWmonitor* monitor = glfwGetPrimaryMonitor();
const GLFWvidmode* mode = glfwGetVideoMode(monitor);

WIDTH = mode-&gt;width;
HEIGHT = mode-&gt;height;

glfwWindowHint(GLFW_RED_BITS, mode-&gt;redBits);
glfwWindowHint(GLFW_GREEN_BITS, mode-&gt;greenBits);
glfwWindowHint(GLFW_BLUE_BITS, mode-&gt;blueBits);
glfwWindowHint(GLFW_REFRESH_RATE, mode-&gt;refreshRate);

window = glfwCreateWindow(WIDTH, HEIGHT, &quot;MV_Game&quot;, monitor, nullptr);
if (!window)
    throw std::runtime_error(&quot;Failed to open GLFW window&quot;);

glfwMakeContextCurrent(window);
glfwSetInputMode(window, GLFW_CURSOR, GLFW_CURSOR_NORMAL);
</code></pre>
<p>Also remove these for real fullscreen:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>glfwWindowHint(GLFW_DECORATED, GLFW_FALSE);
glfwSetWindowPos(window, 0, 0);
</code></pre>
<p>They are for normal windowed windows. A fullscreen window doesnt need decorations disabled manually.</p>
<p>If you actually want fake borderless fullscreen, dont hardcode <code>(0, 0)</code>. Use the monitor position:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>int mx, my;
glfwGetMonitorPos(monitor, &amp;mx, &amp;my);

glfwWindowHint(GLFW_DECORATED, GLFW_FALSE);

window = glfwCreateWindow(WIDTH, HEIGHT, &quot;MV_Game&quot;, nullptr, nullptr);
if (!window)
    throw std::runtime_error(&quot;Failed to open GLFW window&quot;);

glfwSetWindowPos(window, mx, my);
glfwMakeContextCurrent(window);
glfwSetInputMode(window, GLFW_CURSOR, GLFW_CURSOR_NORMAL);
</code></pre>
<p>Hardcoding <code>(0, 0)</code> can be wrong on multi-monitor setups, even if it seems fine most of the time.</p>
<p>The Discord thing also makes me think this is not your input code. Discord often captures the game and cursor as seperate layers. So your friend seeing the cursor while the game looks frozen doesnt really prove that GLFW is hiding it. It sounds more like a Windows/GPU/compositor issue.</p>
<p>I would check these next:</p>
<pre class="lang-cpp prettyprint-override"><code>std::cout &lt;&lt; glfwGetVersionString() &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
std::cout &lt;&lt; glfwGetInputMode(window, GLFW_CURSOR) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
std::cout &lt;&lt; (glfwGetWindowMonitor(window) != nullptr) &lt;&lt; &quot;\n&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>If cursor mode is normal and it still disappears, stop debugging mouse input. Update GLFW, update GPU drivers, and disable overlays like Discord, NVIDIA, AMD, Xbox Game Bar, etc. This looks more like a platform/display bug than a GLFW input bug.</p>
